entional  agricultural systems  so there's uh billions of pounds of  pesticides that are used annually  and and there are consequences to this  over-reliance on pesticides  including non-targets effects on  biodiversity  as well as effects on human health  across the world the main type of  pesticide used  are herbicides and so herbicides are a  class of pesticide  that is used to manage  plants or weeds and the consequence  of over-reliance on herbicides  has been the increase in cases in  herbicide resistance  and since the 1980s we've had a  pre-consistent  and steep increase in weeds that  are not as responsive or not responsive  at all to herbicide treatments  the increase in herbicide resistance is  particularly problematic  when taken into account with the fact  that the agrochemical sector  has not put out a new uh herbicide mode  of action  for the last 30 years so quite literally  farmers can't stay ahead of the  herbicide resistance curve  and in new york herbicide resistance is  a  real issue there are four weeds which  are  officially defined as herbicide  resistant  and their common ground cell smooth  pigweed common ragweed and common  lambsquarters  however there is reported herbicide  resistance  in horse weed tall water hemp and  polymer ammonium  and these are pretty common weeds in new  york and so the fact that there's  herbicide resistance in these species  shows that it's a real problem and it's  a real problem  here in our state  on the flip side of the coin uh a lot of  weed management  in farm systems and especially in  organic systems  is accomplished through tillage however  overuse of tillage drives erosion  in fact most erosion occurs on croplands  and is  due to tillage and erosion  is quite problematic to the  sustainability  of agriculture as about one-third of the  earth's arable land has been  lost to erosion in the last 40 years  okay and so compared to the average  natural area  erosion rates in agricultural settings  are one to 